   "hypothesis_id": "H3",
   "mechanism": "INPP5D is a lipid phosphatase that hydrolyzes PtdIns(3,4,5)P3 into PtdIns(3,4)P2, which negatively regulates PI3K signaling pathways. By depleting PtdIns(3,4,5)P3, INPP5D prevents the optimal recruitment and activation of PLCG2 in microglia. This suppression dampens microglial activation, whereas inhibiting INPP5D relieves the bottleneck, promotes PLCG2 signaling, and confers protection against Alzheimer disease.",
